course: dictionary
lesson: 13532
title: Hard to believe
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Implausible means hard to believe, or not likely to be true. When a story, excuse or idea does not fit the facts, it is implausible. «The dog ate my homework, then flew out of the window.» Nobody thinks a dog can fly, so this excuse is implausible.
explain: Plausible is the opposite. It means believable, or likely to be true. Check a claim by asking two questions: could this really happen, and does the evidence support it?
explain: Implausible can also mean not reasonable or realistic. «His plan to build a castle in one day was implausible.» The plan might sound exciting, but it cannot work in real life. The adverb is implausibly, as in «The story ended implausibly fast.»
explain: Look for the phrases around the word. «An implausible excuse» is one nobody believes. «It seems implausible that» shows doubt: you find something hard to believe. In science and history, ask whether a claim fits the evidence before you accept it.
fact: Implausible means hard to believe, or not likely to be true.
fact: The opposite of implausible is plausible, which means believable.
fact: Implausible can also mean not reasonable or realistic, as in «an implausible plan».
fact: The adverb is «implausibly», as in «The story ended implausibly fast.»
fact: In science and history, check whether a claim fits the evidence before you believe it.
exercise: «His excuse was implausible: he said a seagull ate his whole maths test.» What does implausible mean here? | Easy to read | Quite long | *Hard to believe | Very well written
exercise: Which word means the opposite of implausible? | *plausible | loud | unclear | silly
exercise: «The plan to build a castle in one day was implausible.» Which phrase is closest in meaning? | *Not realistic | Very hungry | Carefully wrapped | Very colourful
test: «The detective did not believe the story, because the timings were implausible.» What does this tell us? | The story was very short | *The story was hard to believe | The detective was very happy | The timings were very neat
test: Which phrase means an excuse that nobody believes? | A happy excuse | *An implausible excuse | A plausible excuse | A long excuse
exam: «Ravi says, "It seems implausible that the moon is made of cheese."» Why might Ravi be right? | The moon is very bright | Cheese is very tasty | Moon cheese is sold in shops | *The evidence shows it is not true
